{"action_id":"a0006","additional_user_input":[],"audience":"unspecified","constraints":[],"context":null,"goal":null,"inputs":{"draft":{"answer":"Let R(x)=(x^2-4)/(x-2). Its domain is the real numbers except 2. Since x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2), for every real x≠2,\n\nR(x)=((x-2)(x+2))/(x-2)=x+2.\n\nThus the simplification is x+2 subject to x≠2. It agrees with the original expression throughout the original domain, but is not equivalent to it at every real number: at x=2 the original expression is undefined, while x+2=4.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator, determine when the denominator is nonzero, and cancel the common factor only on that domain.","id":"conditional-cancellation","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This establishes both the simplified formula and its precise domain."},{"description":"Cancel x-2 and regard x+2 as identical to the quotient on all real inputs.","id":"unconditional-cancellation","outcome":"rejected","reason":"At x=2 the quotient has denominator zero, whereas x+2 is defined."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"For real x where the expression is defined, simplify (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2).\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-expression","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Let R(x)=(x^2-4)/(x-2) for real x where this quotient is defined.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in steps denominator-zero and establish-domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-expression"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of R is {x∈R:x≠2}.","step_ids":["denominator-zero","establish-domain"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step factor-numerator.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"numerator-factorization","kind":"deduct